{"ts": 1789705297.992953, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I've submitted my specification. Summary of my approach:\n\n1. **Screened all 40 features individually** with sign +1, which revealed most legitimate features have modest Sharpe ratios in the range of roughly -0.35 to +0.41 \u2014 consistent with real, noisy return-predicting signals.\n\n2. **Flagged and excluded features 13, 29, and 33** \u2014 these showed absurd in-sample Sharpe ratios (~50) that perfectly flipped sign under sign reversal and combined almost additively (up to ~72 combined). This is not a pattern real financial signals produce; it's the signature of degenerate/leaked features (e.g., encoding forward returns directly or some numerical artifact). Using them would likely catastrophically fail out-of-sample, so I avoided them entirely despite their tempting in-sample scores.\n\n3. **Searched combinations among legitimate features**, focusing on the strongest individual performers (features 18, 1, 8, 34, 5, 30, etc.), testing pairs and triples with various sign combinations.\n\n4. **Found the best legitimate specification**: features [18, 1, 8], all long, giving in-sample Sharpe of 0.637 \u2014 a plausible, robust result (confirmed by checking that alternate sign combinations performed much worse, ruling out a fluke).\n\n5. **Submitted with a conservative OOS estimate** (mean 0.32, sd 0.25), applying a meaningful haircut to the in-sample Sharpe to account for selection bias from testing many combinations and the general tendency of in-sample Sharpe to overstate true out-of-sample performance."}
